-- title:
--   Frobenius on X acts as w⁻¹ under evaluation
-- statement:
--   The setting is a Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation datum together with its evaluation embedding, and the assertion concerns the effect of a Frobenius-type endomorphism on that embedding.
--
--   **Arithmetic base.** A prime $r$; a characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O$ which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), with an irreducible element $\pi$ (`hπ`), $\pi$-adically complete (`hcomplete`), residue ring of cardinality $r$ (`hres`), and absolutely unramified in the sense that $(r) = (\pi)$ as ideals of $\mathcal O$ (`hunr`); a characteristic-zero field $K_0$ which is a fraction field of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **Unramified coefficients.** A characteristic-zero domain $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra, with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, subject to: $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is $\pi$-adically complete (`hOnr_complete`); the ideal generated by $\pi$ is maximal (`hOnr_max`); every element of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ satisfies, modulo $\pi$, a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ (`hOnr_alg`); every monic polynomial over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`); and $\mathrm{Fr}(x) \equiv x^{r} \pmod{\pi}$ for all $x$ (`hFr`).
--
--   **Determinant valuation.** A homomorphism $v\!\det : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) such that $v\!\det(g) = n$ holds precisely when $\det g = u\,\pi^{n}$ in $K_0$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$ (`hvdet`).
--
--   **Group data.** A group $G$, a homomorphism $\sigma : G \to \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ and a subgroup $\Gamma \le G$ such that: some $z \in \Gamma$ has $\sigma z$ a scalar matrix $c \cdot 1$ with $v\!\det(\sigma z) = 2$ (`hcent`); some $w \in \Gamma$ has $v\!\det(\sigma w) = 1$ (`hodd`); and a subgroup $\Gamma'$ whose elements are exactly the $x \in \Gamma$ with $v\!\det(\sigma x)$ even (`hΓ'`). Further, a homomorphism $\rho : G \to \mathrm{PGL}_2(K_0)$ with $\rho g$ the class of $\sigma g$ (`hρ`); the image $\rho(\Gamma')$ acts on the vertices of the lattice tree of $\mathcal O$ in $K_0$ with finite stabiliser sets (`hdisc`) and with finitely many orbits, in the form of a finite set $S$ of vertices meeting every orbit (`hcocpt`).
--
--   **The model.** A scheme $\mathcal X$ with a proper flat morphism $f : \mathcal X \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, and a family of maps $\Theta$: for each $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent, a map from pairs $(\psi, P)$, with $\psi : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to B$ an $\mathcal O$-algebra map and $P$ a Deligne datum over $B$ for $\pi$ (a rule assigning to every full $\mathcal O$-lattice $M \subset K_0^2$ a $B$-submodule $\mathrm{line}(M)$ of $B \otimes_{\mathcal O} M$ with invertible quotient, monotone under inclusions of lattices, compatible with scalar homotheties, and nondegenerate at every prime of $B$), to the set of $B$-points $\{\varphi : \operatorname{Spec} B \to \mathcal X \mid \varphi \text{ followed by } f \text{ is } \operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal O \to B)\}$. The hypotheses on $\Theta$ are: `hΘnat`, naturality in $B$ along $\mathcal O$-algebra maps; `hΘinv`, invariance under the twisted action, namely $\Theta(x') = \Theta(x)$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma$ and `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` holds for $\sigma\gamma$ and the pair $x, x'$, which means $x'_1 = x_1 \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-v\!\det(\sigma\gamma)}$ and $x'_2$ is the pullback of $x_2$ along $(\sigma\gamma)^{-1}$ (for every lattice $M$, $x'_2(\mathrm{line}\,M)$ is the preimage of $x_2(\mathrm{line}\,((\sigma\gamma)^{-1}M))$ under the base-changed lattice isomorphism); `hΘfib`, that for every algebraically closed field $k$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra with $\pi$ nilpotent and every $\psi : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to k$, each $k$-point of $\mathcal X$ over $\mathcal O$ is of the form $\Theta(\psi, P)$, and $\Theta(\psi,P) = \Theta(\psi,P')$ holds precisely when some $\gamma \in \Gamma$ twists $(\psi,P)$ into $(\psi,P')$ in the above sense; and `hΘuniv`, a universal property: for every scheme $T$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and every family $\rho'$ of maps into the nilpotent points of $T$ which is natural and twisted-invariant in the same two senses, there is a family $u$ of maps from the nilpotent points of $f$ to those of $T$ which is natural, satisfies $u \circ \Theta = \rho'$, and is unique with these two properties (any natural $u'$ with $u' \circ \Theta = \rho'$ agrees with $u$ on all $B$ and all points).
--
--   **Analytic frame.** A field $C$ which is a $K_0$-algebra, carrying a valuation with values in a linearly ordered commutative group with zero $\Gamma_0$, complete and algebraically closed; a commutative ring $R$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra and an intermediate algebra for $\mathcal O \to C$, with the relevant scalar towers; a pseudo-uniformizer $\varpi$, i.e. an element $\varpi.\varpi \in K_0$ whose image in $C$ has valuation strictly between $0$ and $1$ and such that every nonzero $a \in K_0$ satisfies $v(\varpi)^{N} \le v(a) \le v(\varpi)^{-N}$ for some $N$; and `hF : IsAdicFrame π ϖ R`, which asserts that $\pi$ is irreducible, $R \to C$ is injective, the image of $R$ in $C$ is exactly $\{c : v(c) \le 1\}$, $R$ is $\pi$-adically complete, the elements of $K_0$ with valuation $\le 1$ in $C$ are exactly those coming from $\mathcal O$, the image of $K_0$ in $C$ is closed, and $\pi$ and $\varpi.\varpi$ have the same image in $C$. Also: an $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi_0 : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to R$; the rank-one condition `hrk` that for $x, y \in C$ with $v(x) < 1$ and $y \ne 0$ there is $n$ with $v(x)^n \le v(y)$; the cofinality condition `hval` that every nonzero $\varepsilon \in \Gamma_0$ dominates the valuation of some nonzero element of $C$; the exhaustion `hex`, that every point of the Drinfeld upper half-plane $\Omega = C \setminus \mathrm{im}(K_0)$ lies in some affinoid $\mathrm{affinoid}\,\varpi\,n$; and the assumption that the ring $\mathcal O(\Omega) :=$ `HolRingOf ϖ ρ`, consisting of those functions $\Omega \to C$ whose restriction to each affinoid is a uniform limit of a uniformly bounded sequence of rational functions without poles there, is a domain.
--
--   **Uniformisation of adic points.** A map $\Phi$ from adic points (compatible systems of Deligne data over the quotients $R/(\pi^{n+1})$) to the set of morphisms $p : \operatorname{Spec} R \to \mathcal X$ with $p$ followed by $f$ equal to $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal O \to R)$, satisfying the four clauses of `hΦ`: (a) for every adic point $x$ and every $n$, reduction of $\Phi x$ modulo $\pi^{n+1}$ coincides with the point $\Theta$ attached to the pair consisting of $\psi_0$ reduced modulo $\pi^{n+1}$ and the $n$-th stage $x.\mathrm{pt}\,n$; (b) $\Phi$ is surjective; (c) $\Phi x = \Phi x'$ holds precisely when $x' = x.\mathrm{act}(\sigma\gamma)$ for some $\gamma \in \Gamma'$; (d) base change to $C$, sending $p$ to $\operatorname{Spec}(R \to C)$ followed by $p$, is injective on these points and hits every $cpt : \operatorname{Spec} C \to \mathcal X$ with $cpt$ followed by $f$ equal to $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal O \to C)$.
--
--   **Generic fibre and evaluation.** A morphism $sC : \operatorname{Spec} C \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ equal to $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\mathcal O \to K_0 \to C$ (`hsC`), with the fibre product $\mathcal X_C := \mathcal X \times_{\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O} \operatorname{Spec} C$ assumed integral; a ring homomorphism $e$ from the function field of $\mathcal X_C$ to the subfield `invariantFieldOf C G (HolRingOf ϖ ρ) Γ'` of $\operatorname{Frac}(\mathcal O(\Omega))$ consisting of the elements fixed by every $\gamma \in \Gamma'$ for the $G$-action. The hypothesis `heval` states that for every open $U$ of $\mathcal X_C$ containing the generic point $\eta$ and every section $s$ over $U$ there are elements $a$ and $b$ of $\mathcal O(\Omega)$, with $b$ a non-zero-divisor, such that the image under $e$ of the germ of $s$ at $\eta$, viewed in $\operatorname{Frac}(\mathcal O(\Omega))$, is the fraction $a/b$, and such that for every adic point $x$, every $z \in \Omega$ with $z$ equal to the coordinate $x.\mathrm{toOmega}\,C$, every $q : \operatorname{Spec} C \to \mathcal X_C$ with $q$ followed by the first projection equal to $\operatorname{Spec}(R \to C)$ followed by $\Phi x$ and $q$ followed by the second projection equal to the identity, and every witness that $q^{-1}(U)$ is all of $\operatorname{Spec} C$, one has $b(z)$ times the element of $C$ corresponding to the global section $q^{*}s$ equal to $a(z)$. The hypothesis `hfin` states that for every such $U$ and every $n$ the set of $z$ in the $n$-th affinoid which are coordinates $x.\mathrm{toOmega}\,C$ of adic points $x$ admitting a $q$ as above with $q^{-1}(U)$ *not* all of $\operatorname{Spec} C$ is finite.
--
--   **Frobenius data.** An endomorphism $b : \mathcal X \to \mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ (`hbf`: $b$ followed by $f$ is $f$) whose effect on nilpotent points is Frobenius on the coefficient component: for every $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent and every pair $x = (\psi, P)$, the natural transformation induced by $b$ sends $\Theta(\psi,P)$ to $\Theta(\psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}, P)$ (`hb`). An endomorphism $b_C$ of $\mathcal X_C$ with $b_C$ followed by the first projection equal to the first projection followed by $b$ (`hbC₁`) and $b_C$ followed by the second projection equal to the second projection (`hbC₂`). Finally an element $w \in \Gamma$ with $v\!\det(\sigma w) = 1$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every open subset $U$ of $\mathcal X_C$, every witness that the generic point $\eta$ lies in $U$, every witness that $\eta$ lies in $b_C^{-1}(U)$, and every section $s$ of the structure sheaf over $U$: the image under $e$ of the germ at $\eta$ of the section $b_C^{*}s$ over $b_C^{-1}(U)$, regarded as an element of $\operatorname{Frac}(\mathcal O(\Omega))$, equals $w^{-1}$ applied, through the $G$-action on $\operatorname{Frac}(\mathcal O(\Omega))$, to the image under $e$ of the germ at $\eta$ of $s$.
--
--   This is the Frobenius compatibility rider for the evaluation embedding of the function field of a Čerednik–Drinfeld quotient into the $\Gamma'$-invariant fractions of holomorphic functions on the Drinfeld upper half-plane: pullback along the coefficient-Frobenius endomorphism of the generic fibre corresponds to the action of an element $w$ of $\Gamma$ of odd determinant valuation. It is used in the assembly of the ring isomorphism between the function field of the base-changed model and the invariant field, where it supplies the Frobenius (descent) part of the structure.
